The code statements that lead to the bug are:
xas_lock_irq(&xas);
mlru = xas_store(&xas, NULL);
xas_unlock_irq(&xas);
...
for_each_node(i)
    memcg_reparent_list_lru_one(lru, i, &mlru->node[i], parent);
Reason:
The xas_store(NULL) clears the xarray entry for the dying memcg before the per‑node lists are reparented. This allows a concurrent list_lru_del() to see xa_load() == NULL, walk to the parent via lock_list_lru_of_memcg(), and take the parent’s per‑node lock while the item is still physically linked on the child’s list. If another thread holds the child’s per‑node lock at the same time, both threads modify the same list’s next/prev pointers under different locks, causing list corruption.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
mlru = xa_load(&lru->xa, memcg->kmemcg_id);
if (!mlru)
    continue;
...
for_each_node(i)
    memcg_reparent_list_lru_one(lru, i, &mlru->node[i], parent);
xa_erase_irq(&lru->xa, memcg->kmemcg_id);
Reason:
The fix reverses the order: first, the per‑node lists are reparented (and the child’s list is marked dead via LONG_MIN inside memcg_reparent_list_lru_one), and only then is the xarray entry erased. Now any concurrent list_lru operation that still sees the xarray entry either acquires the child’s per‑node lock (synchronizing with the drain) or, after reparenting, finds the list marked dead and walks to the parent where the items already live, preventing concurrent modification of the same list under different locks.

